import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Task: dead-code cleanup and the kernel boundary (ADR-0126)","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/tasks/0126-dead-code-cleanup.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/tasks/0126-dead-code-cleanup.md"}`),a={name:`handl/project/tasks/0126-dead-code-cleanup.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="task-dead-code-cleanup-and-the-kernel-boundary-adr-0126" tabindex="-1">Task: dead-code cleanup and the kernel boundary (ADR-0126) <a class="header-anchor" href="#task-dead-code-cleanup-and-the-kernel-boundary-adr-0126" aria-label="Permalink to “Task: dead-code cleanup and the kernel boundary (ADR-0126)”">​</a></h1><p>Status: <strong>A (part 1), C, E done; B and D measured.</strong> Remaining: A part 2 (needs a maintainer call — see below), B (a migration, deserves its own ADR), D (mechanical re-enable). Plan agreed with the maintainer.</p><h2 id="the-principle" tabindex="-1">The principle <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-principle" aria-label="Permalink to “The principle”">​</a></h2><p>ADR-0040: the kernel is only the composition primitives, and <em>everything is a plugin</em>. ADR-0126 pins the boundary:</p><blockquote><p>Rust holds the <strong>kernel</strong> and <strong>generic tooling</strong>. Everything else is <strong>Handl over a C ABI / SDK-gen contract</strong>.</p></blockquote><p>An unwired Rust crate is therefore an <em>idea in the wrong place</em>, not merely dead weight. Retire the reification, keep the idea.</p><p>Maintainer&#39;s binding rulings (this plan is downstream of them):</p><ol><li>The LSP owns <strong>no</strong> formatter of its own — formatting is the generic formatter&#39;s job (<code>plugin-ext-fmt</code>).</li><li>All tooling is Rust for performance and bootstrapping; it will be rewritten in Handl after bootstrap, and is <strong>configured by Handl as data (plugins)</strong>.</li><li>The LSP lives in <code>plugin-lsp</code>. <strong>No duplication</strong> in <code>host-lsp</code>.</li><li><strong>n-dim / n-morphisms are core</strong> to the language. ADR-0119 stays.</li></ol><h2 id="evidence-standard-learned-the-hard-way" tabindex="-1">Evidence standard (learned the hard way) <a class="header-anchor" href="#evidence-standard-learned-the-hard-way" aria-label="Permalink to “Evidence standard (learned the hard way)”">​</a></h2><p>Keep facts and guesses apart; never delete on a heuristic.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Strength</th><th>Example</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Compiler-proven</strong></td><td><code>never used</code> / <code>never read</code> from rustc; verified the live path elsewhere</td><td>delete</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reachability-proven</strong></td><td>nothing depends on the crate (two independent scans)</td><td>retire via the ledger</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Heuristic</strong></td><td>&quot;name appears once repo-wide&quot;; &quot;files look similar&quot;</td><td><strong>no deletion</strong> — census it instead</td></tr></tbody></table><p>An earlier draft listed 68 one-use public functions and four &quot;near-clone&quot; file pairs as cleanup. Both are heuristics: <code>pub</code> is written on purpose, and differing checksums prove only that files differ. They moved to workstream E, read-only.</p><h2 id="workstreams" tabindex="-1">Workstreams <a class="header-anchor" href="#workstreams" aria-label="Permalink to “Workstreams”">​</a></h2><h3 id="a-—-the-lsp-owns-no-indenter-ruling-1-—-part-1-done" tabindex="-1">A — the LSP owns no indenter (ruling 1) — PART 1 DONE <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-—-the-lsp-owns-no-indenter-ruling-1-—-part-1-done" aria-label="Permalink to “A — the LSP owns no indenter (ruling 1) — PART 1 DONE”">​</a></h3><p><strong>Done — range formatting.</strong> <code>crates/host-lsp/src/range_formatting.rs</code> went from a 307-line hand-rolled line-based indenter to a ~85-line <em>projection</em> of the canonical result: format the whole document with <code>format_source</code>, then emit an edit for the selected lines that changed. Its tests were rewritten to assert against <code>format_source</code> (the oracle) rather than hand-written strings, and the divergence this cleanup had surfaced — &quot;format selection&quot; indenting input <code>hnl fmt</code> leaves untouched — is gone: <code>cargo test -p handl-host-lsp</code> is 255 + 16 green.</p><p><strong>Pending — on-type formatting.</strong> <code>crates/host-lsp/src/on_type_formatting.rs</code> (295 lines) is the second hand-rolled indenter. It is <em>not</em> a mechanical delegation: the feature exists to indent a <strong>blank</strong> new line as you type, and <code>format_source</code> only rewrites lines that have content — so swapping it in would quietly disable auto-indent. Two options, needing a maintainer decision:</p><ol><li>give the generic formatter an &quot;indent this blank line&quot; entry point (the canonical indent logic already exists internally), then delegate; or</li><li>drop <code>textDocument/onTypeFormatting</code> from the LSP and rely on format-on-save.</li></ol><p>The same question applies to <code>on_close_bracket</code> (re-indenting a line when a closing bracket is typed) — option 1 covers both.</p><h3 id="b-—-host-lsp-vs-plugin-lsp-an-accepted-adr-partially-executed-—-audited" tabindex="-1">B — <code>host-lsp</code> vs <code>plugin-lsp</code>: an accepted ADR, partially executed — AUDITED <a class="header-anchor" href="#b-—-host-lsp-vs-plugin-lsp-an-accepted-adr-partially-executed-—-audited" aria-label="Permalink to “B — host-lsp vs plugin-lsp: an accepted ADR, partially executed — AUDITED”">​</a></h3><p><strong>The decision already exists.</strong> <code>project/adrs/archived/ADR-LSP.md</code> (<strong>Status: Accepted</strong>) says:</p><blockquote><p><em>&quot;LSP features are implemented as separate plugins … <code>handl-tooling</code> exposes <code>ToolingHost</code>, which coordinates parsing, formatting, diagnostics, and the LSP plugin slots. <code>handl-host-lsp</code> dispatches LSP requests to <code>ToolingHost</code> … It does not know concrete parser or compiler internals. … Formatting and diagnostics are shared with the command-line tooling; they are not duplicated inside the LSP. … <code>handl-host-lsp</code> stays thin: document store, request mapping, and incremental sync.&quot;</em></p></blockquote><p>That is exactly the maintainer&#39;s ruling 1 and 3. So <code>host-lsp</code>&#39;s 14,490 lines are <strong>a divergence from an accepted ADR</strong>, and this workstream is <em>finishing that ADR</em> — no new ADR is needed. <code>project/adrs/ADR-LSP-remaining.md</code> (<strong>Proposed</strong>) is the live work item and names the next two features: <strong>hover</strong> and <strong>source-position resolution</strong>.</p><p><strong>How far the migration got.</strong> <code>ToolingHost</code> exists with plugin slots (<code>with_completion_plugin</code>, <code>with_goto_def_plugin</code>, <code>with_document_symbols_plugin</code>, <code>with_hover_plugin</code>, <code>with_signature_help_plugin</code>, <code>with_folding_range_plugin</code>, <code>with_semantic_tokens_plugin</code>, <code>with_incremental_sync_plugin</code>), and <strong>7 of 26</strong> handler modules dispatch through it: <code>completion</code>, <code>definition</code>, <code>document_symbols</code>, <code>folding_ranges</code>, <code>hover</code>, <code>signature_help</code>, <code>workspace</code> (plus <code>workspace_symbols</code>). The rest are hand-coded in <code>host-lsp</code> with no plugin seam: <code>call_hierarchy</code>, <code>code_actions</code>, <code>code_lens</code>, <code>color</code>, <code>diagnostics</code>, <code>document_highlight</code>, <code>document_link</code>, <code>execute_command</code>, <code>inlay_hints</code>, <code>linked_editing</code>, <code>moniker</code>, <code>references</code>, <code>rename</code>, <code>selection_ranges</code>, <code>type_hierarchy</code> (and the host-infrastructure ones: <code>initialize</code>, <code>lifecycle</code>, <code>formatting</code>, <code>semantic_tokens</code>).</p><p><strong>Two divergences have already been closed</strong> (workstream A, which is this ADR&#39;s <em>&quot;formatting … not duplicated inside the LSP&quot;</em> clause): the second full-document formatter (<code>c60fda0d</code>) and the hand-rolled range formatter (<code>f500a64f</code>).</p><p><strong>Correction to the earlier alarm.</strong> The 16 zero-coverage files in <code>handlers/</code> are <em>thin protocol glue</em> (26–64 lines each) delegating to top-level feature modules that <strong>do</strong> have running <code>*_tests.rs</code> siblings. 0% there means &quot;no test drives the server protocol end to end&quot;, not &quot;16 features are unverified&quot; — and that missing end-to-end test is arguably a <strong>prerequisite</strong> for this migration, since it is the only thing that would catch a feature regressing as it moves behind the seam.</p><p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> finish <code>ADR-LSP-remaining.md</code> (hover next — it already has a slot in <code>ToolingHost</code> and a placeholder plugin), and take the other un-migrated features one at a time: each is either expressible as a <code>Language::lsp</code> rule (move it) or genuinely host-specific (declare it and keep it). Nothing gets deleted on the way.</p><p><strong>Prerequisite DONE.</strong> The end-to-end safety net now exists: <code>crates/host-lsp/src/handlers_e2e_tests.rs</code> drives <strong>23</strong> handlers with a real document on disk and asserts each reply is a well-formed LSP response carrying the request id — covering every one of the 16 previously-uncovered handler modules (highlight, moniker, selection ranges, document link, references, colour, linked editing, code actions, prepare/rename, prepare/sub/super type hierarchy, call hierarchy incoming/outgoing, colour presentation, execute command, inlay hint(s), resolve, signature help, workspace symbols, code lens). <code>host-lsp</code> went from 255 to <strong>278</strong> tests, all passing, and no handler misbehaved — so the 0% coverage was a missing test, not hidden breakage. A feature can now regress loudly when it moves behind the seam.</p><h3 id="c-—-finish-adr-0119-s-cleanup-half-supersession-hard-evidence-—-done" tabindex="-1">C — finish ADR-0119&#39;s cleanup half (supersession, hard evidence) — DONE <a class="header-anchor" href="#c-—-finish-adr-0119-s-cleanup-half-supersession-hard-evidence-—-done" aria-label="Permalink to “C — finish ADR-0119&#39;s cleanup half (supersession, hard evidence) — DONE”">​</a></h3><p>Landed in <code>refactor: finish ADR-0119&#39;s cleanup half</code>. Beyond the four JS lowerers, the same supersession had left a <code>#[cfg(any())]</code>-disabled test module in <code>term_data.rs</code> (458 lines, never compiled since the transition) — replaced by an active round-trip test. A new gate (<code>no_silently_disabled_compilation</code> in <code>crates/platform-tests/tests/wiring.rs</code>) now fails on any empty <code>cfg(any())</code>.</p><p>ADR-0119 states the proof/path forms are subsumed by the n-dimensional calculus:</p><blockquote><p><em>&quot;Dimension-2 arrows: a 2-cell is an arrow <code>f ~[2]~&gt; g</code>. Rewrite rules are just <code>dim=2</code> arrows.&quot;</em><em>&quot;Proof and path forms (<code>Equiv</code>, <code>Path</code>, <code>Witness</code>, <code>Refine</code>, <code>Univalence</code>, <code>Glue</code>, <code>Fibrant</code>, <code>JElim</code>) are represented as <code>Arr</code>/<code>Op</code> terms at the appropriate dimension.&quot;</em></p></blockquote><p>Evidence: <code>Term::Path</code> / <code>Term::Equiv</code> exist <strong>only</strong> in <code>plugin-engine/src/term_data.rs</code> and its own unit tests — no parser, no backend, no construction from source. <code>CellId</code> / <code>Refine</code> / <code>JElim</code> were already deleted by the same migration. <code>plugin-compile-js/src/lower/proof.rs</code> carries <code>lower_path</code> / <code>lower_equiv</code> (and <code>_applied</code> forms) that are never dispatched: the dispatcher matches only <code>Term::Rewrite</code>.</p><p>Therefore: delete <code>Term::Path</code> / <code>Term::Equiv</code>, the four never-dispatched lowerers, and the <code>term_data</code> tests that only round-trip them. <strong><code>Term::Rewrite</code> stays</strong> — it is live in every backend and ADR-0119 merely reinterprets it as a <code>dim=2</code> arrow.</p><h3 id="d-—-n-dim-n-morphisms-—-measured-and-it-is-not-a-feature-project" tabindex="-1">D — n-dim / n-morphisms — MEASURED, and it is not a feature project <a class="header-anchor" href="#d-—-n-dim-n-morphisms-—-measured-and-it-is-not-a-feature-project" aria-label="Permalink to “D — n-dim / n-morphisms — MEASURED, and it is not a feature project”">​</a></h3><p>Measured by running the ignored tests (<code>--ignored</code>). <strong>Result: n-dim is essentially implemented.</strong> The 172 ignored tests are not blocked work; the <code>#[ignore = &quot;n-dim&quot;]</code> labels are obsolete.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>arrow_model_delegate</code></td><td>8 / 8 pass</td></tr><tr><td><code>arrow_model_parity</code></td><td>24 pass, 1 stale</td></tr><tr><td><code>composite_model_parity</code></td><td>24 pass, 1 stale</td></tr><tr><td><code>exec_model_parity</code></td><td>63 / 63 pass</td></tr><tr><td><code>type_model_parity</code></td><td>22 pass, 1 stale</td></tr><tr><td><code>value_model_parity</code></td><td>24 pass, 1 stale</td></tr><tr><td><code>call_args_binding</code></td><td>1 / 1 pass</td></tr><tr><td><code>op_handler_with_e2e</code></td><td>1 stale</td></tr><tr><td><code>option_b_frontier_e2e</code></td><td>1 stale</td></tr><tr><td><code>proc_notation</code></td><td>1 stale</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>166 pass; 6 fail, every one a stale assertion about something ADR-0119/ADR-0074 deliberately changed</strong> — not a missing capability:</p><ul><li><code>parity_cell_comp_v</code> (×3 files) expects a <code>{&quot;__tag__&quot;:&quot;Cell2&quot;, …}</code> record; ADR-0119 §4 folded <code>CellCompV</code>/<code>CellId</code>/<code>Whisker</code> into dimensioned <code>Arr</code>/<code>Op</code>.</li><li><code>type_model_cobind_is_graded_type</code> expects the pre-ADR-0119 <code>Arr</code> shape (<code>{&quot;__tag__&quot;:&quot;Arr&quot;,&quot;value&quot;:&quot;Affine&quot;}</code>); the implementation correctly emits the new one (<code>{&quot;__tag__&quot;:&quot;Arr&quot;,&quot;dim&quot;:{&quot;__tag__&quot;:&quot;Const&quot;,&quot;value&quot;:1},&quot;value&quot;:&quot;Affine&quot;}</code>). The <em>implementation</em> is right — the test predates the <code>dim</code> field.</li><li><code>graded_arrow_lexes_parses_runs</code> uses <code>~[R]~&gt;</code>, removed by ADR-0119 §1.</li><li><code>arrow_x_to_x_evaluates_to_closure</code> (<code>proc_notation</code>) is labelled <em>&quot;function runtime value shape is not yet producing closure records&quot;</em> — <strong>that label is wrong</strong>: closure records are minted (<code>handl-value</code> emits <code>{&quot;__arrow__&quot;: true, &quot;params&quot;: […], &quot;body_def&quot;: …}</code>) and the test fails because <code>x ~&gt; x</code> uses <code>~&gt;</code> surface syntax that ADR-0074 removed.</li></ul><p><strong>Revised scope for D:</strong> delete the obsolete ignore labels, re-enable the 166 tests, and update the 6 stale assertions to the post-fold shapes. That is roughly a day of mechanical work — <em>not</em> the &quot;implement the n-dimensional calculus&quot; project the ignore labels implied. No ADR work needed; ADR-0119 stands.</p><p><strong>EXECUTED.</strong> 169 of the 173 <code>n-dim</code> ignore labels were removed, so those tests now run on every build — <strong>all green</strong> (<code>handl-runtime</code>: <code>arrow_model_delegate</code> 9, <code>arrow_model_parity</code> 24, <code>composite_model_parity</code> 24, <code>exec_model_parity</code> 63, <code>type_model_parity</code> 23, <code>value_model_parity</code> 24, <code>call_args_binding</code> 1, <code>op_handler_with_e2e</code> 1, <code>option_b_frontier_e2e</code> 13; <code>handl-kit-data-canonical</code>: <code>proc_notation</code> 6). Three stale expectations were corrected:</p><ul><li><code>graded_arrow_lexes_parses_runs</code> and <code>op_handler_with_runs_end_to_end</code> used the pre-ADR-0119 graded surface <code>~Addable~&gt;</code>; the current spelling is the bracketed <code>~[Addable]~&gt;</code> (confirmed by <code>handl-kit-arrow-lower</code>, which documents a bare <code>var</code> in <code>~[N]~&gt;</code> as a <em>named</em> dimension).</li><li><code>type_model_cobind_is_graded_type</code> expected the pre-ADR-0119 <code>Arr</code> shape; it now expects <code>dim</code> as well, which is what the implementation correctly emits.</li></ul><p>Three labels remain, each with a <strong>precise</strong> reason instead of the blanket <code>&quot;n-dim&quot;</code>, because they are genuine open questions rather than blocked work:</p><ol><li><code>arrow_parity_cell_comp_v</code>, <code>parity_cell_comp_v</code> (×2 files) — a <strong>model-parity gap</strong>: for a derived (<code>Term::Opaque</code>) record the Handl model evaluates it (<code>{&quot;__tag__&quot;:&quot;Cell2&quot;, …}</code>) while the Rust default model returns <code>Null</code>. Recording it rather than picking a side: ADR-0119 §4 says <code>CellCompV</code> is redundant once dimensions exist, so which model is &quot;right&quot; is a design call.</li></ol><p><strong><code>arrow_x_to_x_evaluates_to_closure</code> — resolved, and it corrected me.</strong> Its label claimed <em>&quot;function runtime value shape is not yet producing closure records&quot;</em>. I first dismissed that as wrong (reasoning that <code>~&gt;</code> was removed by ADR-0074), then tested it: <code>~&gt;</code> is alive — <code>grammar.arrow.infix</code> parses it into a <code>function</code> node and <code>crypto.hnl</code> writes <code>name :: (params) ~&gt; body</code> throughout — but neither <code>x ~&gt; x</code> nor <code>(x) ~&gt; x</code> evaluates to a <code>__arrow__</code> record. A probe showed what it actually returns:</p><div class="language-json"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">json</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">{</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#C5C5C5;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">Op</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">:{</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#C5C5C5;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">args</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">:[],</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#C5C5C5;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">dim</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">:{</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#C5C5C5;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">Const</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">:{</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#C5C5C5;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">value</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">:</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">1</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">}},</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#C5C5C5;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">name</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">:</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;">id</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">,</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#C5C5C5;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">source</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#005CC5;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">:</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;">Native</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">}}</span></span></code></pre></div><p>So <strong>arrow literals evaluate to serialized <code>Term</code> records</strong> (the arrow-lower pipeline, ADR-0119/ADR-0120), and the identity folds to the <code>id</code> combinator. The original label was substantively right, and the test now asserts the real behaviour (<code>arrow_x_to_x_evaluates_to_the_identity_op</code>, re-enabled).</p><h3 id="e-—-census-of-the-one-use-public-functions-read-only-—-done" tabindex="-1">E — census of the one-use public functions (read-only) — DONE <a class="header-anchor" href="#e-—-census-of-the-one-use-public-functions-read-only-—-done" aria-label="Permalink to “E — census of the one-use public functions (read-only) — DONE”">​</a></h3><p>Scope: <code>pub fn</code> whose identifier appears <strong>exactly once</strong> in the whole repo (definition only). Current count: <strong>67 across 37 files</strong> (the original sweep said 68; the retired crates accounted for almost none of it — the list was mostly live-crate API surface). No deletions on this evidence alone: <code>pub</code> is written on purpose, and a name used nowhere yet is not the same as a name that is useless.</p><p>The families, and where each belongs under the maintainer&#39;s rulings:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>n</th><th>What it is</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>kit-canonical</code> classifiers (<code>is_*_kind</code>, <code>category_of</code>, …)</td><td>11</td><td>token classification as free functions over embedded YAML text</td><td><strong>checked — 8 are unused, 2 are live.</strong> <code>is_identifier_kind</code> (5 uses) and <code>is_number_kind</code> (4) are wired, which is exactly the pair <code>AGENTS.md</code> describes as classifying <em>embedded</em> YAML text where no <code>Language</code> is available; the other eight (<code>is_string_kind</code>, <code>is_bool_kind</code>, <code>is_null_kind</code>, <code>is_comment_kind</code>, <code>is_doc_comment_kind</code>, <code>is_operator_kind</code>, <code>is_delimiter_kind</code>, <code>is_template_kind</code>) appear <strong>once each — at their own definition</strong>. They are a family written as a set and only half wired, and they hardcode canonical <code>token.*</code> ids when <code>Language::token_in_category</code> is the data-driven equivalent (ADR-0081). Decision for the maintainer: retire the eight unused siblings (the family&#39;s live purpose is served by the two used helpers plus the <code>Language</code> API), or un-wire nothing and keep them</td></tr><tr><td><code>platform</code> host/VM API (<code>with_globals</code>, <code>collect_vars</code>, <code>env_mut</code>, <code>get_plugin</code>, …)</td><td>11</td><td>the kernel&#39;s own public surface (ADR-0031/0040)</td><td><strong>kernel API</strong> — keep, but likely wants an <code>api</code> module or <code>#[doc(hidden)]</code>; only a maintainer should shrink the kernel boundary</td></tr><tr><td><code>host-tooling</code> (<code>run_cli</code>, <code>read_source_or_stdin</code>, <code>semantic_tokens_legend</code>, <code>with_*_plugin</code>)</td><td>5</td><td>CLI + LSP plumbing seams</td><td><strong>generic tooling</strong>: check whether <code>host-cli</code> reimplements <code>run_cli</code> — if so, that is the ruling-2 &quot;no duplicate&quot; case applied to tooling, and <code>host-cli</code> should call it</td></tr><tr><td><code>*_handl_plugin()</code> constructors (crypto, kv-hashmap, otlp, otlp-std, parse, serialize, tag, ip)</td><td>8</td><td>one <code>HandlLibraryPlugin</code> ctor per FFI library — identical in shape</td><td><strong>unused, and every library is registered by another path</strong> — but <em>not</em> via one uniform replacement: <code>crypto</code>/<code>parse</code>/<code>tag</code> register a <code>*Module</code> type through <code>kit-canonical</code>; <code>kv-hashmap</code>/<code>otlp</code>/<code>otlp-std</code> go through <code>plugin-ext-kit::library_registry</code>; <code>serialize</code>/<code>parse</code> through <code>plugin-lib-stdlib::ops::*</code>. Each is <code>pub</code> API of its own crate, so retiring the eight is a per-crate decision, not one switch</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-compile-*</code> emit/ABI internals (<code>emit_program</code>, <code>emit_placeholder</code>, <code>dwarf_line_table</code>, <code>build_apply_native_call</code>, <code>expect_tag</code>)</td><td>6</td><td>backend internals; <code>dwarf_line_table</code> + the 0%-covered <code>emit/debug_info.rs</code></td><td><strong>an unwired idea</strong>: debug info for compiled output. Keep and record — do not delete</td></tr><tr><td><code>handl-language</code> fragment builders (<code>with_faces</code>, <code>with_keyword_clauses</code>, <code>with_trailing_tokens</code>, <code>with_trivia</code>, …)</td><td>6</td><td>programmatic construction API for language data (ADR-0044/0081/0096)</td><td><strong>capability API</strong> — the repo builds <code>Language</code>s from YAML, but <code>with_trailing_tokens</code> backs a real ADR-0096 feature. Keep</td></tr><tr><td><code>host-lsp</code> internals (<code>hover_at</code>, <code>property_at_offset</code>, <code>cross_arrow_hover</code>, <code>invalidate</code>, <code>invalidate_dir</code>, <code>rest_of_line</code>)</td><td>6</td><td>hover variants + kit-resolver cache invalidation</td><td><strong>fold into workstream B</strong> (the <code>host-lsp</code> vs <code>plugin-lsp</code> audit)</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-engine*</code> / transform (<code>with_rule_source</code>, <code>run_pass_root</code>, <code>child_is_kind</code>, <code>remap_children_from</code>, <code>op_native_dim</code>, <code>fixpoint_trace</code>)</td><td>6</td><td>engine entry points; <code>op_native_dim</code> is a dimensioned <code>Term</code> ctor</td><td><strong><code>op_native_dim</code> belongs to D (n-dim)</strong>; the rest are engine API — keep</td></tr><tr><td>singletons (<code>is_enabled</code>, <code>has_plugin_id</code>, <code>from_arc</code>, <code>set_partial</code>, <code>with_tag</code>, <code>new_with_project</code>, <code>with_caps</code>, <code>with_factories</code>, <code>with_resolver_fallback</code>, <code>into_vm_error</code>, <code>invalid_argument</code>, <code>fixpoint_trace</code>)</td><td>12</td><td>one-offs across the workspace</td><td>case-by-case; none is urgent</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Outcome:</strong> no deletions. Six of the eight families turn out to be <em>live API or a recorded idea</em>, not garbage — which is exactly the correction the maintainer asked for. Two families are candidates for a maintainer decision, and neither is a bulk switch:</p><ul><li>the <code>kit-canonical</code> token classifiers: ADR-0081 puts classification in <code>Language</code> as data (<code>token_in_category</code>), so these are misplaced rather than dead — but first check how much <code>token_in_category</code> already covers;</li><li>the eight <code>*_handl_plugin()</code> constructors: unused, and every library <em>is</em> registered, but through three different mechanisms (<code>kit-canonical</code> <code>*Module</code>, <code>plugin-ext-kit::library_registry</code>, <code>plugin-lib-stdlib::ops::*</code>). Retiring them is a per-crate call.</li></ul><p><strong>A note on process:</strong> this census replaced an earlier claim that the eight constructors were &quot;superseded — one decision retires all eight&quot;. Checking each crate showed that was too glib (only three register a <code>*Module</code>). The same happened twice more in this task (the &quot;68 useless functions&quot;, the &quot;16 unverified LSP features&quot;). Heuristics produce leads; each one needs its own verification before anything is deleted.</p><h2 id="order" tabindex="-1">Order <a class="header-anchor" href="#order" aria-label="Permalink to “Order”">​</a></h2><p><strong>A + C → E (census) → B (audit) → D (measure, then implement).</strong></p><p>A and C are deletions resting on hard evidence. E and B are reading. D is the feature and needs scoping first.</p><h2 id="done-so-far-on-main-pushed" tabindex="-1">Done so far (on <code>main</code>, pushed) <a class="header-anchor" href="#done-so-far-on-main-pushed" aria-label="Permalink to “Done so far (on main, pushed)”">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>53a558bd</code> — recovered 5 silently-nested tests in the NBE synthesiser (pre-existing brace defect; they had never run).</li><li><code>2a68cfab</code> — retired 14 unwired crates (~2.9k LOC) behind <code>project/parked.yaml</code>; added the wiring gate <code>crates/platform-tests/tests/wiring.rs</code>; removed the vestigial duplicate <code>KitManifest</code> + <code>from_manifest</code> shim. Members 102 → 87.</li><li><code>c60fda0d</code> — deleted <code>host-lsp</code>&#39;s superseded second formatter (318 lines + <code>TextEdit</code> + 9 tests), dropped a dead helper, cleared 22 unused imports.</li><li>Six <code>parked/*-2026</code> tags pushed, so every retirement is one <code>git checkout</code> away.</li></ul>`,58)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};